houses  and if you get there if you get to our  community  the water have gone above the zip level  situation of course and the pressures on  areas of Nigeria are set to intensify  with the minister of humanitarian  Affairs warning that five states are  still at risk of experiencing floods up  until the end of November  now MPS from across different political  parties are calling on the government to  treble the amount of money it's planning  to spend on Energy Efficiency now the  recent mini budget included spending an  extra 1 billion pounds on insulation for  low-income households to protect them  from the spiraling gas prices or Sky  Saudia Chaudhary uh joins me now hi  Saudia so what's the latest from the  government on this then  well the government has always insisted  that it remains committed to reaching  Net Zero carbon emissions by 2050 but  recently it has been criticized by  campaigners who say that it it appears  to be rowing back on those commitments  by policies like the lifting of the ban  on fracking and granting more oil and  gas exploration licenses in the North  Sea now the all-party Parliamentary  group is urging the government to show  more commitment by trebling the amount  it has pledged to spend on Energy  Efficiency so in the mini budget as you  just said the government had laid out  plans to spend an extra one billion  pounds on insulating low-income  households the appg wants to see that  number increase to three billion pounds  and this is part of its 10 point plan  for climate in the environment sort of  list of priorities that the government  should address in the next 12 months and  so what it wants is what it calls a  retrofit Revolution to address those  insulation concerns in homes it says  that will not only create jobs in the  country but will also permanently reduce  energy bills but it also wants to see  things like a cut in vat at public  charging points for electric vehicles  from 20 to 5 it wants to see more  funding for example for decarbonizing  agriculture so a list of things that the  appg has put forward as it urges the  government to spend more on addressing  Energy Efficiency and all of this comes  of course as the government is urging  all of us to do more to reduce the  amount of energy we use this winter so  things like switching off a light when  you leave the room or switching off  devices which would perhaps otherwise be  left on standby and you might remember  the boss of off gem last week talking  about more technical things that we can  all do like reducing the flow  temperature in our boilers which he said  perhaps won't reduce energy bills for us  individual customers but can help  address the energy supply issues that  the country faces this winter but the  government appears to think perhaps  there aren't those energy supply issues  going on yesterday the climate Minister  Graham Stewart said that the UK is in in  a pretty good position for supplies but  he failed to rule out energy rationing  this winter admitting that the risk of  blackouts is higher this year than in  previous years okay Saudia Chaudhary  thank you very much good to have you on  the show  time now for a look at some of the day's  other climate news for you and one third  of the world's 100 largest private firms  with revenues totaling nearly 1.2  trillion dollars don't have plans to  reduce carbon emissions that's compared  with over two-thirds of the largest 100  publicly listed companies Now new data  released by Net Zero tracker also found  that none of the top 10 private  companies have a net zero Target and  it's led to calls for enhanced  transparency and a drive for more action  in the private sector  now the Sunderland air show has been  canceled indefinitely as the council  looks to make the city carbon neutral  now the popular events which attracted  hundreds of thousands of Spectators was  last held in 2019 but hasn't gone ahead  since the pandemic well the councils say  their ambition is for the city to reach  carbon neutral status by 2040 and  therefore have no plans to run the air  show in the future  just stop oil protesters are still  blocking the M25 Dartford Crossing  demanding the government halts all new  oil and gas licenses and consents for  the Queen Elizabeth II Bridge which  links Kent to Essex was closed on Monday  after two people from the group climbed  to the top they say they'll remind  remain on top of the bridge until  they're brought down  now expanding access to clean energy  sources is crucial to The Climate crisis  but according to the new  multi-dimensional poverty index there  are still 600 million people who don't  have access to electricity and clean  cooking fuel now research by the United  Nations development program and the  Oxford poverty and human development  initiative found that improving access  is crucial for people and the planet  well earlier I spoke to Professor Sabina  akaya the director of the Oxford poverty  and human development initiative at  University of Oxford  the poor people that we look at are  facing a Nexus of different deprivations  that Collide that are bundled together  and a number of them are linked to  climate crisis it could be nutrition and  problems of rising food prices of food  insecurity lack uh lack of access and it  could also be as we've seen electricity  it could also be having good housing  materials and so in a sense there is a  need to address their situation with  providing the clean energy the climate  policies can do and in the sense also by  supporting them you are supporting  people living on the most vulnerable  parts of the planet and also therefore  having livelihoods that can change their  lives and also change the well-being of  the surrounding environments and it's  been a while we have you here cop27 it's  around the corner what would you like to  hear from this year's climate conference  in Egypt we are so conscious of the  climate crisis and simultaneously at